{
  "trojan_detected": true,
  "suspect_instances": [
    {
      "instance_name": "u_cmp_ff",
      "cell_type": "DFF_NEGEDGE",
      "sampled_signal": "grant_pre",
      "justification": "design_brief.md documents a single clocking discipline in which every state element in access_ctrl_top is instantiated as DFF_POSEDGE, sampling on the rising edge of clk. Cross-referencing access_ctrl_netlist.v shows that the instance u_cmp_ff is instantiated as DFF_NEGEDGE, capturing its .d input on the falling edge of clk instead of the rising edge. The .d port of u_cmp_ff is tied to the net grant_pre, which is the direct output of the comparator AND2 gate u_and_final and represents the raw, unregistered access-permission decision produced by the XOR2/INV/AND2 comparison tree against REF_KEY. Because u_cmp_ff samples grant_pre a half clock period earlier than the documented posedge discipline would dictate, it can latch a stale or transitional value of the comparator output before the rest of the posedge-synchronous pipeline has settled, opening a timing window through which an incorrect permission value can propagate into the registered grant_out output."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "A single DFF_NEGEDGE substitution was found on instance u_cmp_ff, which samples the access-permission comparator output grant_pre on the falling edge of clk instead of the rising edge used by every other documented state element, violating the single posedge clocking discipline specified in design_brief.md and creating a half-cycle window for a stale grant_pre value to reach grant_out."
}

